WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.790
- [Mark] All right, Chris.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:06.623
- Yes.

00:00:06.623 --> 00:00:07.456
- [Mark] Chris, where'd you grow up?

00:00:07.456 --> 00:00:08.289
Where are you from originally?

00:00:08.289 --> 00:00:11.570
- I'm from here.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:12.820
- [Mark] Which is Leslie County.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.030
- Leslie County, yeah.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.970
Middle, this is called,
they call it Middle Fork.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.060
- [Mark] Tell me about your childhood.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.310
You had both your parents
when you were a kid?

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.320
- Yeah, but they never was there for me.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:23.153
- [Mark] Where were they?

00:00:23.153 --> 00:00:24.770
- I kind of raised myself.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.200
- [Mark] What happened to mom and dad?

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.070
- Uh, well, my mom was on drugs,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.543
and my dad, he was, chose a woman over me.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.438
- [Mark] So you ended up, who raised you?

00:00:35.438 --> 00:00:37.703
- Myself and my grandma.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.013
- [Mark] And how would you
describe your childhood?

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:43.873
- It was okay.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.463
- [Mark] What was the roughest part of it?

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.170
- Probably not having a dad.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.770
- [Mark] Yeah, that would do it.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.159
How far do you go in school?

00:00:56.159 --> 00:00:59.603
- I went all the way up to the 12th grade.

00:01:01.238 --> 00:01:02.071
- [Mark] Did you graduate high school?

00:01:02.071 --> 00:01:03.333
- No, I didn't graduate.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:09.310
I went, you know, half
the year in 12th grade.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.730
- [Mark] Oh, I see. So you almost made it.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.683
- Yeah. Almost.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:15.260
- [Mark] And then you went
on your own and did what?

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.720
What kind of jobs have you had?

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.730
- Well, I've been self employed.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.881
I do odd jobs, or I get out and dig roots,

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.093
you know, pull bark.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:26.893
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:01:26.893 --> 00:01:29.210
- Or weed eat, just stuff like that.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.410
- [Mark] So, this is a
very poor part of Kentucky,

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:32.920
which is a very poor part of the country.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.753
- Yes.

00:01:33.753 --> 00:01:37.159
- [Mark] What kind of money
do you make a year doing that?

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:40.313
- Not much. Just barely enough to get by.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.630
- [Mark] But you live in a,
you have a trailer, or you...?

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.060
- Nah, I live, I stay with my dad.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.130
We have a little, it's a
one room, two story house.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.537
- [Mark] Okay. And how old are you?

00:01:53.537 --> 00:01:54.960
- I'm 31.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:55.793
- [Mark] Thirty-one.

00:01:55.793 --> 00:01:57.270
Do you have a wife, or
kids, or anything like that?

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.300
- Yeah. I have a five-year-old boy.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.200
- [Mark] And you're raising him?

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.393
- No, I don't get to see him.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.120
- [Mark] You and his mother separated?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.680
- Yeah, me, his mom told me
he wasn't mine and stuff,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.102
and yeah, I just never have, you know,

00:02:15.102 --> 00:02:17.350
got to have anything to do with him.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.880
- [Mark] Right. When you were a young man

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.580
did you have dreams of doing
something with your life?

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.263
- Yeah. You know,

00:02:27.263 --> 00:02:32.263
I woulda liked to have
played professional sports

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.673
or something like that.

00:02:33.673 --> 00:02:35.040
Baseball or basketball.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.590
- [Mark] Uh-huh. And
have you ever traveled?

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.349
Have you ever been outside of Kentucky?

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:43.703
- Yeah.
- [Mark] Where have you been?

00:02:43.703 --> 00:02:47.993
- Let's see, I've been to
Florida, Michigan, Ohio.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.840
Been to Virginia, North
Carolina, Alabama, Louisiana.

00:02:55.244 --> 00:02:57.380
I've never been past the Mississippi,

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.660
or across the Mississippi.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.240
- [Mark] Yeah. You've been around.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.285
Have you ever considered leaving?

00:03:02.285 --> 00:03:03.740
- Yes.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.280
- [Mark] It seems like
there's very few opportunities

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.960
for work here.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.306
- Yeah, there's not much around here.

00:03:09.306 --> 00:03:13.080
- [Mark] But what keeps
you from packing up

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.516
and moving somewhere else?

00:03:14.516 --> 00:03:16.002
- This is home.

00:03:16.002 --> 00:03:17.034
- [Mark] Home is home.

00:03:17.034 --> 00:03:18.083
- Yeah.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.210
- [Mark] And do you have
friends and family here

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.930
that you stay in contact with?

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.930
- Yeah, my mom, she's from
Bledsoe in Harlan County,

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.470
which isn't far from here.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.470
- [Mark] No, right down the street.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.303
- Yeah.

00:03:38.149 --> 00:03:39.780
Then, on my dad's side,

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.701
I'm kin to almost everyone around here.

00:03:42.701 --> 00:03:43.951
- [Mark] Right.

00:03:45.028 --> 00:03:47.069
What's the best, what are
the best and worst things

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:49.319
about living in Appalachia?

00:03:50.427 --> 00:03:51.260
- Uh,

00:03:54.145 --> 00:03:56.963
the worst, I don't, they're really not,

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.930
none of them (indistinct)
things you could say

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.882
is the best things about
living around here.

00:04:01.882 --> 00:04:02.932
(Chris chuckling)

00:04:02.932 --> 00:04:03.765
- [Mark] It's beautiful.

00:04:03.765 --> 00:04:04.680
- [Chris] I guess family would be it.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:05.690
- [Mark] Yeah. It's beautiful here.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.630
- [Chris] Yeah, it is beautiful.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.880
And you know, you can
get out and, you know,

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:11.283
make money in the mountains.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:13.940
- [Mark] What are the,
what are the worst parts

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:14.773
of living here?

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:20.523
- [Chris] Everything's so
far away, everyone's poor.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.196
- [Mark] A lot of drugs here?
- [Chris] Yeah.

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:25.012
- [Mark] Yeah. Are you
involved in that too?

00:04:25.012 --> 00:04:28.850
- [Chris] I used to be on
drugs, but I'm on Suboxone now.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:29.683
- [Mark] Oh, you are? Okay.

00:04:29.683 --> 00:04:31.533
Yeah, a lot of people use that to get off.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.120
But you're happy here?

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.510
- [Chris] Sometimes.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.640
- [Mark] You're depressed sometimes too?

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:38.473
- [Chris] Yeah.

00:04:38.473 --> 00:04:39.750
- [Mark] Yeah. What gets you down?

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.640
Just, get lonely, or do you get...?

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:43.473
- [Chris] Yeah. And, you know,

00:04:43.473 --> 00:04:45.720
and there not being anything to do.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.710
- [Mark] Right. Do you know
anything about your family?

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.519
The generations that came before you?

00:04:52.519 --> 00:04:53.400
- [Chris] Yeah.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.400
On my dad's side, there,
my granny's brothers.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.310
were banker robbers
back in the 40s and 50s.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:04.143
- [Mark] Really?

00:05:04.143 --> 00:05:04.976
- Yeah.

00:05:04.976 --> 00:05:06.566
You know, they decided that, you know,

00:05:06.566 --> 00:05:10.480
or they might've even started
during the Great Depression.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.080
I'm not sure, but, you know,

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.700
they decided that they
wanted to get away from here,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:15.533
you know.

00:05:15.533 --> 00:05:16.670
- [Mark] Where'd they go?

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.839
- And robbing the banks, I
guess, is how they got by.

00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:21.080
- [Mark] And where'd they
move? Where'd they move?

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:21.913
- Do what?

00:05:21.913 --> 00:05:22.992
- [Mark] Where did they move?

00:05:22.992 --> 00:05:23.825
- Where did they move?

00:05:23.825 --> 00:05:27.873
Well, they robbed banks from
here all the way to New York.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.790
And they got, you know, they got captured

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:32.880
and got put in prison,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.880
and they escaped Federal Prison
in Lewisburg, Pennsylvania.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.690
And, you know, they went on a crime spree

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:40.730
from there to New York.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:45.103
And they got gunned down by,
you know, New York City Police.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.762
It's on the internet.

00:05:47.762 --> 00:05:48.595
- [Mark] Really?

00:05:48.595 --> 00:05:52.590
- Yeah, it's called, Joseph and
Ballard Nolan was her names.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.080
- [Mark] And these were your? Your?

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:55.541
- My great uncles.

00:05:55.541 --> 00:05:59.380
- [Mark] So, you know, living
in Appalachian, you know,

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.840
it's a very, it's a very
tough life, very strong.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.610
You know, you have to
work hard to survive.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:04.443
- Yeah.

00:06:04.443 --> 00:06:06.120
- [Mark] But it makes,
it makes everyone here,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.250
everyone here seems very
honest, and very hardworking,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.940
and very willing to help their neighbor,

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:11.773
and help people.

00:06:11.773 --> 00:06:14.300
- Yeah. They say your word's
all you got. You know?

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.320
- [Mark] Yeah. And people here

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:16.770
seem to have a lot of integrity.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:17.603
- Yeah.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:19.593
- [Mark] You don't see that

00:06:19.593 --> 00:06:21.240
everywhere you go in the country.

00:06:22.342 --> 00:06:24.640
You know, with all the
technological advances

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.190
that are going on in the world,

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.500
Appalachia seems to be
kind of left behind.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:30.333
- Yeah, we are.

00:06:30.333 --> 00:06:32.633
- [Mark] Do you feel like
this is a dying way of life?

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.420
- Uh, no.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:36.357
- [Mark] You think this will continue?

00:06:36.357 --> 00:06:37.538
- Yeah.

00:06:37.538 --> 00:06:38.705
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.770
You, I mean, like for your
son, you have a son, right?

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:43.603
- Yeah.

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:45.743
- [Mark] You see him
staying here like you did?

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.820
- No, he lives out in the
western part of Kentucky,

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:50.753
in Columbia.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.420
You know, and his mom, there,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.810
she got killed back a few years ago.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:59.840
But he stays with her
mom and she's a doctor,

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.884
and you know, he don't have
to want for nothing. So.

00:07:01.884 --> 00:07:02.934
- [Mark] Yeah, so, he's taken care of?

00:07:02.934 --> 00:07:03.767
- Yeah.

00:07:05.817 --> 00:07:07.850
- [Mark] Is this a very simple life?

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:08.683
- Yeah.

00:07:08.683 --> 00:07:11.550
- [Mark] Very, I mean,
what stresses do you have?

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:13.140
What do you worry about,

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.863
or what are you stressed out about?

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:23.030
- Now it's how I'm gonna
get, make, you know,

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:24.870
my next dollar.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:25.703
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.930
It seems like a lot of the people here

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:31.890
are involved in drugs.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:35.462
It seems like they get,
they get maybe bored.

00:07:35.462 --> 00:07:36.295
What do you think the causes

00:07:36.295 --> 00:07:38.173
of the addiction stories you see?

00:07:38.173 --> 00:07:40.093
- Mostly boredom.

00:07:42.173 --> 00:07:43.006
- [Mark] It just gets kind of, yeah.

00:07:43.006 --> 00:07:45.850
- You know, there's nothing
else to do around here,

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:46.683
but get high.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.660
- [Mark] The painkillers were
really the beginning of it,

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:51.090
right?

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:52.840
- Yeah. You know, they was.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.680
Pain pills really aren't
a big thing anymore.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.491
- [Mark] So, crystal meth has taken over?

00:07:57.491 --> 00:08:01.873
- Yeah. Well, it's ice, it's
not really crystal meth.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.220
It's a lot weaker than crystal meth,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.133
but it is methamphetamine.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:15.790
- [Mark] Can you imagine what
your life would have been like

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:19.163
if you had been born in a big
city like Chicago or Atlanta,

00:08:19.163 --> 00:08:20.830
something like that?

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.883
- No, not really.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.220
No, you know, this is....

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.370
- Have you been to some
of the larger cities?

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.270
- Yeah. I used to live in Richmond.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.628
- [Mark] Okay. How'd you like that?

00:08:35.628 --> 00:08:36.760
- I liked it.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.260
I was in high school
the winter I, you know,

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:40.800
lived down there.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.903
I went to a model laboratory school,

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:45.970
it's on the EKU Campus.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.160
It's a private school.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:47.993
- [Mark] Uh-huh.

00:08:51.412 --> 00:08:54.172
- I had a lot in, you know,

00:08:54.172 --> 00:08:57.260
or I could have probably went somewhere

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.320
if I would have stayed at there. But....

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:00.830
- [Mark] You're still a young man.

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:02.247
You still have many years ahead of you.

00:09:02.247 --> 00:09:03.802
You can do many different things.

00:09:03.802 --> 00:09:05.468
- Right.
- [Mark] Do you have any plans

00:09:05.468 --> 00:09:07.062
for anything you'd like to do?

00:09:07.062 --> 00:09:09.892
Or do you wanna just stay here
and dig up roots and ginseng?

00:09:09.892 --> 00:09:14.892
- Well, yeah, you know,
I'd like to get a job,

00:09:14.951 --> 00:09:16.810
you know, a steady paying job.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.473
But, you know, everything's so far away.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.420
- [Mark] What's the most important lesson

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:26.820
you've learned in your life?

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:35.242
- Probably always be honest.

00:09:35.242 --> 00:09:38.197
And, you know, karma
always comes back on you.

00:09:40.203 --> 00:09:43.360
Or, you reap what you
sow, as the Bible says.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:44.801
- [Mark] Are you religious?

00:09:44.801 --> 00:09:46.340
- Yeah, I believe in God.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.983
But I wouldn't say, you
know, I'm really religious.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:52.631
- [Mark] Religion seems to be
important to a lot of people

00:09:52.631 --> 00:09:53.600
in this region.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.083
- Yeah, it is.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.470
You know, they believe
women's gotta wear skirts,

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.320
and long sleeves, and all that.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:06.003
And I don't, you know, I
don't believe that way.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:09.413
And some people handle snakes.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:13.343
- [Mark] Are you Pentecostal?

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:18.333
- Nah, just I went to Church of God.

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:20.483
No, I don't believe, you know,

00:10:20.483 --> 00:10:23.010
women should have to wear a
long sleeves and all that.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.061
That's Pentecostal. That's
the way they believe.

00:10:25.061 --> 00:10:26.950
- [Mark] That's part of the
Pentecostal Church, is it?

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:31.950
- Yeah. I guess you would
just say I'm a Christian.

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:36.183
- [Mark] All right, Chris.

00:10:36.183 --> 00:10:37.320
Well, thank you so much
for talking with me.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:38.153
- [Chris] All right, thank you.

00:10:38.153 --> 00:10:39.470
- [Mark] And good luck with
wherever your life goes

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.303
from here.

00:10:40.303 --> 00:10:41.167
- [Chris] All right.

00:10:41.167 --> 00:10:42.000
- [Mark] Thank you, man.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.583
- [Chris] All right, thank you.

